        "create_time": 1778566737.214,
        "update_time": 1778566744.233251,
        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "翻译：\"\"1. The perturbation operator critically determines ASS\r\nsign—and zero-masking creates artifacts. A preregistered\r\n8-run validity battery (Table XI) tested three operators on\r\nResNet (CLBBB/QRS and 1AVB/PR) plus an SE-ResNet\r\nsentinel. Under zero-masking, CLBBB ASS= −0.010 (95%\r\nCI [−0.011, −0.008]) because ST-segment occlusion causes\r\nan artifactually large AUROC drop (∆ = 0.706). This is a\r\nhard-boundary artifact: zero-masking creates a sharp signal\r\ndiscontinuity that convolutional filters respond to strongly,\r\nindependent of the physiological content of the occluded\r\nregion. Linear interpolation eliminates the boundary and re\r\nstores interpretable behavior: ST ∆AUROC drops to 0.166\r\nwhile QRS target ∆AUROC remains stable at 0.322, yield\r\ning CLBBB ASS= +0.083 (95% CI [0.073, 0.094]). Beat\r\nconditioned inpainting is too conservative to discriminate\r\n(all deltas ≈ 0). The validity battery therefore (i) identifies\r\nlinear interpolation as the preferred operator for matched\r\ncontrol perturbation studies, (ii) reveals that the previously\r\nreported “ST-segment universal disruptor” finding is largely\r\na zero-masking artifact, and (iii) demonstrates that operator\r\nsensitivity is itself a diagnostic—conclusions that depend on\r\nthe perturbation operator are fragile and should not be treated\r\nas evidence of pathway specificity.\"\""
          ]
        },
